<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=dynamite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dynamite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dynamite is an explosive made of nitroglycerin, an absorbent such as clay or diatomaceous earth, and stabilisers, invented in 1866 by the Swedish chemist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>, who stabilised nitroglycerin with earth from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> near his factory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and patented in 1867. Nitroglycerin, first synthesised by Ascanio Sobrero in 1847, had been too sensitive to shock for commercial use. Dynamite was the first safely manageable explosive stronger than black powder and rapidly gained wide-scale use, though it was never popular in warfare; Nobel named it from the Greek dýnamis, &quot;power&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dynamite.jpg" alt="Dynamite" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olecrab · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caisse_dynamite_nobel_paulilles_expo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>'s father, Immanuel Nobel, was an industrialist, engineer and inventor who built bridges and buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. His construction work led him to look for ways of blasting rock that were more effective than black powder. At 17 Alfred was sent abroad for two years and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> studied under the chemist Théophile-Jules Pelouze, who cautioned him against using nitroglycerin as a commercial explosive because it was so sensitive to shock. Nobel invented the detonator, or blasting cap, which allowed a controlled explosion to be set off from a distance, and in 1863 he made his first successful detonation of pure nitroglycerin. On 3 September 1864 his brother Emil and several others were killed in an explosion at the factory at Heleneborg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Despite the blasting cap, nitroglycerin was too unstable for commercial use. Nobel tried to combine it with a substance that would make it safe to handle without lessening its force. Mixtures with cement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and sawdust failed, but diatomaceous earth, which is fossilised algae, stabilised it. He obtained patents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 7 May 1867 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> on 19 October 1867. Dynamite is usually sold as cardboard cylinders about 200 mm long and 32 mm across, and is rated by its weight strength, the share of nitroglycerin it contains, usually from 20 to 60 per cent. It weeps nitroglycerin over time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Dynamite rapidly gained wide use as a safer alternative to black powder and nitroglycerin. Nobel controlled his patents tightly and shut down unlicensed makers, though a few American businessmen avoided the patent by using other absorbents such as resin. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> it was first made by the Giant Powder Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. For several decades from the 1940s the largest producer in the world was the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> established a factory at Somerset West in 1902 to supply the gold mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>.</p><p>Dynamite has never been popular in warfare, because it degenerates quickly under severe conditions and can be set off by fire or a stray bullet. The German armed forces adopted TNT as a shell filling in 1902.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dynamite” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His government built a more professional civil service, guided from 1888 by the State Secretary Willem Johannes Leyds, but it also sold monopolies, notably the <b>dynamite</b> concession, that angered the mining industry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained the only single by an Asian artist to top the chart until &quot;<b>Dynamite</b>&quot; by the South Korean band BTS in 2020, and it has been described as a metaphor for Japan's emergence on the world stage after the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sukiyaki">Sukiyaki (Ue o Muite Arukō)</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfred Nobel, the Swedish chemist who invented <b>dynamite</b>, died in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
